  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A Roman hill is made of 53 million broken jars. Thirty-five metres high, nearly a kilometre around. All of it thrown away.",
        "visual_query": "roman amphora sherd mound",
        "visual_fallback": "broken pottery"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Every jar carried olive oil from southern Spain to feed the city. Around six billion litres. At the peak, 130,000 jars a year.",
        "visual_query": "andalusian olive groves spain",
        "visual_fallback": "olive trees"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Rome recycled almost everything. Broken pottery normally went back into concrete. So why smash these and carry the pieces uphill?",
        "visual_query": "ancient roman brick ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "roman ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Oil-soaked clay wrecks concrete, and these jars broke into curved slabs too big to crush. Dumping them was simply cheaper.",
        "visual_query": "terracotta shards close detail",
        "visual_fallback": "clay fragments"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So Rome engineered its dump. Stepped terraces, retaining walls of whole jars, lime for the stink. Painted labels on the pots still record every weight and inspector.",
        "visual_query": "testaccio rome hill street",
        "visual_fallback": "rome hillside"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
    "word_count": 112,
    "duration_seconds": 35.0,
    "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
    "tts_rate": "+8%",
